A||||13||nette||help! need advice/opinions - which fiero to buy?||||5||
Z||000000||nette||11-20-2001||02:52 PM||||I know this is my decision but I would really appreciate some "expert" advice and opinions to help me decide.  I've found two great cars and now I can't decide which one to go for.<P>1) 87 GT, new paint, 68k miles, automatic, tinted windows, garaged, needs new tires, excellent shape, everything works, $3500, would cost $945 to ship to my location - max cost to me would be $4500<P>2) 86 GT, 70k miles, 5 spd, original paint has minor pitting on hood, small paint cracks in nose,  rt power mirror not working, minor oil leak from a drive shaft seal, needs right lower control arm bushing, engine has minor oil leak, left 1/4 window cracked, tires good, $3200, would cost about $500 to fly out and drive it back home - max cost to me would be $3700<P>I really wanted an 87 or 88, but I also really wanted a 5spd.<P>Please please, if anyone would be gracious enough to help me out here I would be forever in their debt.  Thanks!<P>nette<p>[This message has been edited by nette (edited 11-20-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||JohnF||11-20-2001||03:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by nette:<BR><B>I really wanted an 87 or 88, but I also really wanted a 5spd.<P>[This message has been edited by nette (edited 11-20-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If that's what you want, don't buy either of these - wait for the RIGHT car- you'll find it!<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1578096&a=12001112&p=56509969&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||12
Z||000002||sqoach||11-20-2001||03:02 PM||||Hmm...sounds like the 87 is in much better shape...if nothing is really wrong with it. That's probably what I would go for. Where are these cars located? Maybe a forum member could help you out? Worth a shot. Maybe not have to spend as much getting one or the other.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigsqoach2.gif">              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">               <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||11-20-2001||03:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B> If that's what you want, don't buy either of these - wait for the RIGHT car- you'll find it!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh yeah. That's a good idea too. If you really want a 5 spd, I'd pass on both. The 86 doesn't sound like it's nice enough to pay that much.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||11-20-2001||03:14 PM||||86s and 87s are, for all intents and purposes, nearly identical. By the time you buy the 86, and have everything fixed, you'll have nearly another $1500 in expenses. (Airline ticket and paint/body work will be at least $1000.) <P>For the 87, count on $300-$400 for tires and be sure to consider "stuff you don't know about yet."<BR>On the other hand, if you really don't want an auto...<P>Don't get in a hurry. While both of these are reasonable, neither is a real bargain. If you wait, you may find something even better. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||NEPTUNE||11-20-2001||03:56 PM||||Its usually cheaper to buy a good car than to fix up a rough one. Take into consideration how the cars got to be in the condition they are in today. The 87 appears to have been cared for, the 86 much less so. <BR>In the long run the better cared for vehicle will cause fewer headaches. Auto cars seem to have fewer problems,but 5 spd.is faster and more fun. Good Luck! ||||reg||6
Z||000006||Pheagey||11-20-2001||04:07 PM||||I agree, wait for the right one (mmm 5 speeds forward..)<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed.<BR>"The car formerly know as Dj's Fiero."||||reg||2
Z||000007||nette||11-20-2001||04:25 PM||||OK, then, thank you all so much! That really helps. And the automatic isn't that big of deal to me.<P>With that said, the car is located in Holly, Michigan (50 miles north of Detroit). I am located in Helena, Montana. I could either ship it ($945, and I would have to pick it up in Post Falls, ID) or I could fly back ($600+) and drive it back.<P>Anyone have a better idea?  Thanks.<P>nette||||reg||1
Z||000008||4UMLA1||11-20-2001||05:34 PM||||Dont buy either one of them, wait until you can find an 88 GT, those are the best ones, or at least I think so.  They are quick, have great stock suspension, and are the best looking Fieros made.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.algonet.se/%7Eschumi/index_lights.gif">                    <BR>                   <IMG SRC="http://fosagold.org/ikonboard/images/fosabrand32c.gif">              <BR>The International Formula One Supporters Association      <IMG SRC="http://www.danbbs.dk/~kethmer/ferrari/champione-ani.gif">   <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.danbbs.dk/~kethmer/ferrari/formular1-spinner.gif">  <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000009||K2m4||11-21-2001||02:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by nette:<BR><B>I know this is my decision but I would really appreciate some "expert" advice and opinions to help me decide.  I've found two great cars and now I can't decide which one to go for.<P>1) 87 GT, new paint, 68k miles, automatic, tinted windows, garaged, needs new tires, excellent shape, everything works, $3500, would cost $945 to ship to my location - max cost to me would be $4500<P>2) 86 GT, 70k miles, 5 spd, original paint has minor pitting on hood, small paint cracks in nose,  rt power mirror not working, minor oil leak from a drive shaft seal, needs right lower control arm bushing, engine has minor oil leak, left 1/4 window cracked, tires good, $3200, would cost about $500 to fly out and drive it back home - max cost to me would be $3700<P>I really wanted an 87 or 88, but I also really wanted a 5spd.<P>Please please, if anyone would be gracious enough to help me out here I would be forever in their debt.  Thanks!<P>nette<P>[This message has been edited by nette (edited 11-20-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>i wouldnt buy either. My 86 GT i bought pretty much trashed for $2200. The only good thing was that the motor was rebuilt ond only had anout 5000 miles on it. Well after putting in a new interier ( replaced EVERYTHING) new tires new front and rear suspension, new brakes front and rear and a paint job i spent almost another $4000. Yes i have a sweet GT BUT if i had to do it over id buy one that i could spend 5000 for and just drive it and not have to worry about fixxing it like i did for the fiorst year and a half!<BR>For that cost Fiero dave in Wisconsin always has GTs in that price range and he goes through them top to bottem...Just my .02 cents<P>Kerm <BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
